WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.497
A Transformer is a neural network architecture that turns a sequence into useful representations by letting its pieces exchange information.

00:00:08.497 --> 00:00:14.924
Today, we will follow one tiny message through the machine, and calculate attention ourselves.

00:00:14.924 --> 00:00:19.300
You only need multiplication, addition, and a little curiosity.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.049
Consider the word bank.

00:00:21.049 --> 00:00:24.926
In the river bank, it means the land beside water.

00:00:24.926 --> 00:00:29.792
In the bank approved my loan, it means a financial institution.

00:00:29.792 --> 00:00:31.736
The spelling stays the same.

00:00:31.736 --> 00:00:34.652
The surrounding words change the meaning.

00:00:34.652 --> 00:00:39.720
A Transformer builds a different representation of bank in each context.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:43.758
A tokenizer splits text into pieces called tokens.

00:00:43.758 --> 00:00:48.200
A token can be a word, part of a word, or punctuation.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:51.593
The exact split depends on the tokenizer.

00:00:51.593 --> 00:00:53.799
Each token gets an integer ID.

00:00:53.799 --> 00:00:59.241
That ID looks up a learned vector: a list of numbers called an embedding.

00:00:59.241 --> 00:01:03.580
Our drawings use a few numbers; real models use many more.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:07.506
Imagine an embedding as a location in a high dimensional space.

00:01:07.506 --> 00:01:12.617
Training adjusts these locations so the network can use them to make predictions.

00:01:12.617 --> 00:01:16.789
Do not read one coordinate as a neat label like river or money.

00:01:16.789 --> 00:01:22.020
Useful information is usually spread across many coordinates and interactions.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:29.972
Self attention without position information treats a reordered input as the same collection, with its outputs reordered too.

00:01:29.972 --> 00:01:32.538
It needs a signal about sequence order.

00:01:32.538 --> 00:01:40.473
The original Transformer adds positional vectors to embeddings, using sine and cosine waves at different frequencies.

00:01:40.473 --> 00:01:43.390
Other designs encode position differently.

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:49.020
For our tour, token vector plus position vector gives the starting representation.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:57.023
In self attention, every position makes a request and compares it with information offered by other positions in the same sequence.

00:01:57.023 --> 00:01:59.896
The result is a weighted blend of information.

00:01:59.896 --> 00:02:07.705
A token can gather useful context from a distant token directly, instead of passing everything along one neighbor at a time.

00:02:07.705 --> 00:02:11.373
Each input vector is transformed into three vectors.

00:02:11.373 --> 00:02:14.760
The query is what this position is looking for.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.287
A key is what another position can be matched by.

00:02:18.287 --> 00:02:21.532
A value is the information it can contribute.

00:02:21.532 --> 00:02:26.854
These are helpful analogies, not literal questions written in English.

00:02:26.854 --> 00:02:34.684
The vectors come from learned matrix multiplications: Q equals X times W Q, and similarly for K and V.

00:02:34.684 --> 00:02:40.728
A matrix multiplication forms new coordinates by weighted sums of the old ones.

00:02:40.728 --> 00:02:46.772
Here, the input one, two becomes the query one, zero with this example matrix.

00:02:46.772 --> 00:02:50.402
Training learns the entries of those projection matrices.

00:02:50.402 --> 00:02:57.089
The input changes from sentence to sentence; the learned matrices stay fixed during ordinary generation.

00:02:57.089 --> 00:03:01.228
Different layers and attention heads have their own projections.

00:03:01.228 --> 00:03:08.922
Now forget the words for a moment and use three synthetic candidates, A, B, and C.

00:03:08.922 --> 00:03:11.174
Our query is one, zero.

00:03:11.174 --> 00:03:15.771
The keys are two, zero; zero, two; and one, one.

00:03:15.771 --> 00:03:18.672
We also give each candidate a value vector.

00:03:18.672 --> 00:03:22.383
These invented numbers are chosen for easy arithmetic.

00:03:22.383 --> 00:03:26.229
They are not measurements from a trained language model.

00:03:26.229 --> 00:03:32.279
For A, multiply one times two and zero times zero, then add.

00:03:32.279 --> 00:03:34.095
The score is two.

00:03:34.095 --> 00:03:36.717
For B, the score is zero.

00:03:36.717 --> 00:03:38.532
For C, it is one.

00:03:38.532 --> 00:03:44.206
A larger query key dot product means greater compatibility in the learned representation.

00:03:44.206 --> 00:03:50.199
It is not automatically a probability, and it depends on vector lengths as well as direction.

00:03:50.199 --> 00:03:53.620
Our keys have two coordinates, so d k is two.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:57.116
Divide every score by the square root of two.

00:03:57.116 --> 00:04:03.577
We get approximately one point four one four, zero, and zero point seven zero seven.

00:04:03.577 --> 00:04:04.296
Why scale?

00:04:04.296 --> 00:04:12.347
With many roughly independent, zero mean, unit variance coordinates, dot product variance grows with dimension.

00:04:12.347 --> 00:04:19.033
Scaling helps prevent softmax from becoming excessively sharp and making learning difficult.

00:04:19.033 --> 00:04:24.480
Softmax exponentiates each score and divides by the sum of those exponentials.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:30.136
For A, e to the power one point four one four is about four point one one three.

00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:34.196
The three exponentials total about seven point one four one.

00:04:34.196 --> 00:04:41.640
Our weights are roughly fifty seven point six percent, fourteen percent, and twenty eight point four percent.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:43.873
Every query gets its own recipe.

00:04:43.873 --> 00:04:51.267
Multiply each value vector by its attention weight, then add the results coordinate by coordinate.

00:04:51.267 --> 00:04:55.039
A contributes about one point one five two, zero.

00:04:55.039 --> 00:04:58.585
B contributes zero, zero point two eight zero.

00:04:58.585 --> 00:05:03.001
C contributes zero point two eight four to each coordinate.

00:05:03.001 --> 00:05:09.439
The final output is approximately one point four three six, zero point five six four.

00:05:09.439 --> 00:05:12.508
It is a new vector, not a selected word.

00:05:12.508 --> 00:05:22.105
Attention of Q, K, and V equals softmax of Q times K transpose, divided by the square root of d k, all multiplied by V.

00:05:22.105 --> 00:05:29.108
Transpose turns keys into columns so matrix multiplication computes every query key comparison.

00:05:29.108 --> 00:05:32.130
Softmax runs separately across each row.

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:36.257
Multiplying by V blends values for every query at once.

00:05:36.257 --> 00:05:41.040
For n tokens, Q and K each have n rows and d k columns.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:44.692
Their product gives an n by n score grid.

00:05:44.692 --> 00:05:47.300
V has n rows and d v columns.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:52.121
The final output has n rows and d v columns: one new vector per token.

00:05:52.121 --> 00:05:55.426
Attention weights describe information routing.

00:05:55.426 --> 00:06:00.522
They are not guaranteed explanations of why a model reached a conclusion.

00:06:00.522 --> 00:06:04.051
One attention head can make only one blend per position.

00:06:04.051 --> 00:06:11.170
Multiple heads use different learned projections, so they can gather different kinds of information in parallel.

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:15.729
Their outputs are concatenated, then mixed by another learned matrix.

00:06:15.729 --> 00:06:22.732
Heads can discover different patterns, but we do not assign each one a fixed job like grammar or meaning.

00:06:22.732 --> 00:06:27.065
A Transformer block also contains a feed forward neural network.

00:06:27.065 --> 00:06:36.409
Attention mixes information across positions; this network transforms each position separately, using the same weights at every position.

00:06:36.409 --> 00:06:42.527
A common form expands the vector, applies a nonlinear activation, then projects it back.

00:06:42.527 --> 00:06:49.411
Nonlinearity lets the network build more complex features than a single matrix multiplication can.

00:06:49.411 --> 00:06:53.473
Residual connections add a sublayer's update to its input.

00:06:53.473 --> 00:06:58.586
Instead of replacing the whole representation, each stage can refine it.

00:06:58.586 --> 00:07:01.948
This also helps gradients flow during training.

00:07:01.948 --> 00:07:07.270
Layer normalization rescales a token's features and learns a scale and offset.

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:14.775
The original Transformer normalizes after the residual addition; many variants normalize before the sublayer.

00:07:14.775 --> 00:07:17.709
Stack blocks to repeatedly refine context.

00:07:17.709 --> 00:07:21.782
An encoder can let every input position see the full input.

00:07:21.782 --> 00:07:26.130
A decoder for next token generation uses a causal restriction.

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:31.376
The original Transformer combines an encoder and a decoder for translation.

00:07:31.376 --> 00:07:37.433
In that combination, cross attention uses decoder queries with encoder keys and values.

00:07:37.433 --> 00:07:40.775
Self attention uses one sequence for all three.

00:07:40.775 --> 00:07:45.858
The same basic attention machinery supports different information flows.

00:07:45.858 --> 00:07:51.110
To predict the next token after the cat, the decoder must not see that next token.

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:57.645
A causal mask blocks attention to later input positions by setting their scores to negative infinity.

00:07:57.645 --> 00:08:01.165
After softmax, blocked positions get zero weight.

00:08:01.165 --> 00:08:05.548
A position may still attend to itself and earlier positions.

00:08:05.548 --> 00:08:11.296
Training shifts the targets by one: each position predicts the following token.

00:08:11.296 --> 00:08:19.592
The final representation at the last position is projected into one score, or logit, for every token in the vocabulary.

00:08:19.592 --> 00:08:24.193
Another softmax turns these scores into next token probabilities.

00:08:24.193 --> 00:08:27.453
The system chooses a token, perhaps by sampling.

00:08:27.453 --> 00:08:29.831
It appends that token and repeats.

00:08:29.831 --> 00:08:38.320
These vocabulary probabilities are different from attention weights, which choose how to mix information inside the network.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:44.051
During next token training, the model receives text and predicts the following tokens.

00:08:44.051 --> 00:08:52.047
Cross entropy penalizes giving low probability to the observed next token: loss equals negative log of its probability.

00:08:52.047 --> 00:08:58.771
If the correct token gets probability zero point two, the loss is about one point six zero nine.

00:08:58.771 --> 00:09:02.904
At zero point eight, it is about zero point two two three.

00:09:02.904 --> 00:09:09.698
Backpropagation computes gradients; an optimizer updates learned weights to reduce average loss.

00:09:09.698 --> 00:09:19.705
During training, all the correct input tokens are already available, so many positions can be processed in parallel, while a mask prevents cheating.

00:09:19.705 --> 00:09:24.235
During generation, each new token depends on the previous choices.

00:09:24.235 --> 00:09:27.609
Full attention compares every pair of positions.

00:09:27.609 --> 00:09:32.107
Double the sequence length, and the number of pairs quadruples.

00:09:32.107 --> 00:09:39.416
Efficient implementations reduce memory costs, but long context still requires substantial computation.

00:09:39.416 --> 00:09:51.534
Here is the journey: tokenize, look up embeddings, add position information, then pass through blocks of attention and feed forward computation with residual connections and normalization.

00:09:51.534 --> 00:09:57.280
For attention, remember four verbs: compare, scale, normalize, blend.

00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:00.694
Learned weights make the recipes useful.

00:10:00.694 --> 00:10:08.855
For generation, project the final vector to vocabulary probabilities, choose a token, and repeat.

00:10:08.855 --> 00:10:10.175
One final puzzle.

00:10:10.175 --> 00:10:15.375
Keep our three keys and values, but change the query to zero, one.

00:10:15.375 --> 00:10:19.411
Which candidate gets the greatest attention weight?

00:10:19.411 --> 00:10:22.283
Pause and work out the dot products.

00:10:22.283 --> 00:10:25.770
B now wins: the scores are zero, two, and one.

00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:32.289
The output flips to approximately zero point five six four, one point four three six.

00:10:32.289 --> 00:10:38.353
Attention is a context dependent recipe, recalculated from the current vectors.

00:10:38.353 --> 00:10:41.764
Try it yourself in the companion playground.

00:10:41.764 --> 00:10:44.818
You have now computed the heart of a Transformer.

00:10:44.818 --> 00:10:52.981
It is a network that learns how to route and transform information, building representations that help it predict or solve a task.

00:10:52.981 --> 00:10:56.243
Fluent predictions are not a guarantee of truth.

00:10:56.243 --> 00:11:05.483
But the mechanism is something you can understand: vectors, comparisons, weighted sums, and learned transformations, repeated at scale.
